  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$",
